data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":1731,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0c846378cb8d64a9016adb1a4d1e4c8191bc62c7593e918962","call_id":"call_7e93a6e5f5f444baac4d47182d1ceb98","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"PROOF_CANDIDATE\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"For positive integers n,m, jointly smooth u:R^n×(0,∞)→R^m and fixed unit q∈R^m satisfying |u|=1, u_tt−Δ_xu=a u pointwise for some real scalar a(x,t), and local-in-time uniform compact support of u−q, prove e(·,r)=|u_t|²+Σ_j|∂_j u|² is Lebesgue integrable for each r>0, E(r)=∫_{R^n}e(x,r)dx is differentiable at each r>0, and E'(r)=0. Norms are Euclidean and the gradient square is the Frobenius square. No claim is made at t=0.\\\",\\\"proof\\\":\\\"Fix an arbitrary r>0. First, choose a closed interval J⊂(0,∞) whose interior contains r. By the support hypothesis there is a compact C⊂R^n such that u(x,t)=q for x∉C and all t∈J. On the open set (R^n\\\\\\\\C)×int(J), u is constant, so u_t and every ∂_j u vanish there. Thus for t∈int(J), e(·,t) is a continuous function supported in C. It is bounded on C, since C is compact and e is continuous. A compact subset of R^n is Lebesgue measurable and has finite measure; consequently e(·,t) is Lebesgue integrable. In particular E(t)=∫_C e(x,t)dx for t∈int(J).\\n\\nThe function e(x,t) is smooth. Its time derivative is\\n∂_t e=2 u_t·u_tt+2Σ_{j=1}^n (∂_j u)·(∂_j u_t).\\nOn C×J this derivative is continuous, hence uniformly continuous. For h→0 with r+h∈int(J), the fundamental theorem of calculus gives, for x∈C,\\n[e(x,r+h)−e(x,r)]/h = ∫_0^1 ∂_t e(x,r+θh)dθ.\\nUniform continuity on C×J implies that this quotient converges uniformly in x∈C to ∂_t e(x,r). Since C has finite measure, the difference between the integrals of the quotient and of ∂_t e(·,r) is at most |C| times the uniform difference, and tends to zero. (If C has measure zero this bound still applies.) The integrands e(·,r+h), e(·,r), and ∂_t e(·,r) vanish off C, so this proves differentiability of E at r and E'(r)=∫_C ∂_t e(x,r)dx.\\n\\nDifferentiate |u(x,t)|²=1 in t and in each spatial coordinate. This gives u·u_t=0 and u·∂_j u=0, and differentiating the latter identity once more in x_j gives |∂_j u|²+u·∂_{jj}u=0. Summing over j yields u·Δ_xu=−Σ_j|∂_j u|². Taking the dot product of u_tt−Δ_xu=a u with u, and using |u|²=1, therefore gives\\n−|u_t|²+Σ_j|∂_j u|²=a.\\nThus the scalar in the equation is necessarily a=Σ_j|∂_j u|²−|u_t|². Also u·u_t=0, so the equation implies pointwise\\nu_t·u_tt=u_t·Δ_xu.\\n\\nFor completeness, spatial integration by parts here has no boundary term: for each j, the smooth vector fields ∂_j u(·,r) and u_t(·,r) vanish outside C and are compactly supported. Applying the one-dimensional integration-by-parts formula in the x_j variable and Fubini's theorem componentwise gives\\n∫_{R^n}(∂_j u)·(∂_j u_t)dx=−∫_{R^n}(∂_{jj}u)·u_t dx.\\nAll integrands are compactly supported and continuous, so these integrals exist. Using the derivative formula for E and summing these identities now gives\\nE'(r)=2∫_{R^n}u_t·u_tt dx+2Σ_j∫_{R^n}(∂_j u)·(∂_j u_t)dx\\n=2∫_{R^n}u_t·Δ_xu dx−2∫_{R^n}u_t·Δ_xu dx=0.\\nSince r>0 was arbitrary, the integrability, differentiability, and conservation conclusions hold at every required time.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Extreme value/boundedness theorem for continuous functions on compact sets; compact subsets of Euclidean space are Lebesgue measurable and have finite Lebesgue measure.\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem (elementary topology/measure)\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"e is continuous on compact C; support in C makes the integral finite. This only establishes integrability, not differentiation or conservation.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fundamental theorem of calculus, applied to the smooth time function t↦e(x,t), plus uniform continuity of ∂_t e on compact C×J.\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem (elementary calculus)\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"C×J is compact and lies in the open domain in time. The displayed FTC identity and uniform convergence of the difference quotients are proved in the argument; finite measure of C then justifies convergence of integrals. No unverified general differentiation-under-the-integral theorem is being assumed.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fubini's theorem and one-dimensional integration by parts for continuously differentiable compactly supported functions, applied componentwise in each spatial coordinate.\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem (elementary integration)\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"∂_j u(·,r) and u_t(·,r) are smooth and supported in C; all relevant products and derivatives are integrable and compactly supported. Fubini reduces the R^n identity to the one-dimensional compact-support identity, whose boundary term is zero.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Candidate mathlib source probes for a later Lean handoff: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Bochner.Basic (Bochner integral and integrability declarations); Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.FundThmCalculus (FTC candidates); Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Bochner.Fubini (Fubini candidates). Exact declarations/signatures and the task's mathlib pin were not available in the supplied documents and were not source-verified in this stage.\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"candidate library references; not invoked as verified premises\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"Later source work must inspect exact pinned source files, full declaration signatures and hypotheses, and establish equivalence of the library integral with the stated Lebesgue integral. These candidates are not evidence of a theorem application or Lean acceptance.\\\"}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":\\\"The proof is local around an arbitrary interior time r>0, choosing J⊂(0,∞); it makes no assertion at 0. The compact set C may depend on J, as allowed. It is not assumed that u itself is compactly supported: only its derivatives and e are supported in C because u equals the fixed q off C. The scalar a requires no regularity; its value is derived pointwise from the sphere constraint and the equation. The Frobenius sum is kept componentwise throughout.\\\",\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"The natural-language argument uses only the explicitly stated hypotheses and elementary standard theorems cited above. A formalization should register exact source declarations for any imported calculus/integration facts; no pinned Lean/mathlib version or commit is supplied in the frozen task documents, and no source probe was performed in this stage.\\\",\\\"This is a proof candidate only, not NL0/Audit A acceptance or Lean/Audit B acceptance.\\\"]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

